      "The Namib is a coastal desert running along the south-western edge of Africa, with its main body inside Namibia. Its aridity is produced by the South Atlantic subtropical high and amplified by cold upwelling in the Benguela Current: a hot desert pressed directly against a cold ocean surface, a combination that is uncommon worldwide. According to planning documents submitted with the UNESCO nomination, the desert-and-coast protected belt along the Namibian coastline runs about 1,570 km, and its inland width ranges from about 25 km at the narrowest to about 180 km at the widest.",
    formation:
      "The origin of the aridity is tied to the break-up of Gondwana: about 130 million years ago the Southern Ocean gateway opened, Antarctica moved towards the pole, and the Benguela Current was set going. The nomination dossier states that the sand sea and the underlying Tsondab Sandstone have not been wetter than semi-arid for roughly 130 million years, and that the modern Namib was firmly established at least 32 million years ago. The sand here is not local: it comes from exorheic river systems far inland to the south-east, relayed by three conveyor belts — river transport, a northward longshore current, and onshore wind — over a distance of more than 1,000 km.",
