      "Compared with the inland deserts already in this library, such as the Taklamakan and the Simpson, the Namib differs fundamentally in being a cold-current coastal desert whose moisture comes from fog rather than rain. The nomination dossier offers its own comparison: the Namib is not unique in its aridity, and the Atacama has been dry since at least the Miocene. Further east, the Kalahari is grass-covered semi-fixed sand, whereas the Namib combines mobile sand with stony gravel desert.",
