      "Spitzkoppe is a cluster of granite peaks on the Namib gravel plains, made up of Gross Spitzkoppe, Klein Spitzkoppe and the Pontok Mountains, standing 600 to 700 m above the plain as a group. The sheer granite wall of Gross Spitzkoppe draws climbers, which earned it the nickname Matterhorn of Africa.",
    formation:
      "It belongs to the same family as Brandberg and Erongo: a tectono-magmatic episode about 135 million years ago intruded granite into schists and marbles of the Damara Supergroup around 650 million years old, and into the younger Karoo sediments above them. That intrusion was preceded by an intensified phase of basaltic to rhyolitic magmatism, which left behind a dense swarm of north-north-east trending dykes. Several million years of erosion then stripped the Karoo and Damara country rock away layer by layer, carving the granite into what stands today.",
    observation:
      "The first cue for identifying it is not the mountain but the dykes: a set of dark, straight north-north-east trending ridges crosses the gravel plain and is conspicuous on aerial photographs and satellite images, and following them leads straight to this cluster of granite peaks. The three groups of peaks are spread across several kilometres of gravel plain with flat ground between them, and the rock carries the same reddish tone.",
    distinguish:
      "The difference from Brandberg is one of bulk and form: Brandberg stands more than 1,800 m above the plain as a single massif with its own summit climate zone, while this rises only 600 to 700 m as a cluster of peaks. The difference from Erongo is one of rock assemblage: Erongo combines intrusive and extrusive rocks, is about 35 km across and is ringed by a dyke, whereas Spitzkoppe is a small, purely plutonic body.",
    concept:
      "These rounded peaks were not carved by wind. Granite develops concentric joints as the magma cools and solidifies, and weathering then peels it away along those joints layer after layer, producing rounded boulders and smooth rock faces — a process known as onion-skin weathering, or exfoliation. Wind and the extreme day-night temperature range only finish the work; they are not the cause. The natural rock bridge, weathering pits and boulder fields here all come from the same mechanism.",
    history:
      "Klein Spitzkoppe is one of Namibia's well-known mineral localities: for more than a century local people have won topaz, aquamarine and green fluorite and other semi-precious stones from small workings in the pegmatites and their weathering products. The rock art site known as Bushman's Paradise, at the eastern end of the Pontok Mountains, holds national monument status. The name Pontok comes from the thatched huts built by local people — their rounded roof shape is exactly what early visitors were reminded of on seeing these hills.",
